  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381c28",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "ASO Titre ",
  "code": "ASO T",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>ASO Level (IU/mL)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Levels</div></td><td><div>Normal ASO levels are typically less than 200 IU/mL for adults and less than 300 IU/mL for children. Normal levels indicate no significant recent streptococcal infection or the resolution of a past infection.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Elevated ASO levels above the normal range suggest a recent or ongoing streptococcal infection. The level of ASO antibodies in the blood usually starts to rise about a week after the onset of a streptococcal infection and peaks within 3 to 6 weeks. The magnitude of the elevation does not necessarily correlate with the severity of the infection.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Decreasing Levels</div></td><td><div>A declining trend of ASO levels over time indicates that the streptococcal infection is resolving, and the body's immune response is effectively controlling the infection. It can also be a useful indicator to monitor the response to antibiotic treatment.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Persistent High Levels</div></td><td><div>Persistently high ASO levels may be seen in some individuals who have had a recent streptococcal infection or those with chronic streptococcal carriage. In some cases, a chronic or recurrent streptococcal infection can lead to post-infectious complications, such as rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis. Monitoring ASO levels may be beneficial in assessing the risk of these complications.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
